1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.881
Welcome to Module Four —

2
00:00:02.394 --> 00:00:03.621
advanced training. This

3
00:00:04.301 --> 00:00:07.211
is where you learn to optimize the way

4
00:00:07.832 --> 00:00:10.669
the papers do: better optimizers,
learning-rate schedules, regularization,

5
00:00:10.669 --> 00:00:13.505
and fast data pipelines. Start with the
optimizer

6
00:00:14.411 --> 00:00:16.163
zoo. For vision, plain SGD

7
00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:19.375
with momentum still beats Adam
surprisingly often.

8
00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:21.892
Adam adapts a per-parameter learning rate
for

9
00:00:22.526 --> 00:00:26.165
you, and AdamW fixes it by decoupling
weight decay from

10
00:00:26.165 --> 00:00:29.805
the gradient step. But here's the thing —
your learning-rate

11
00:00:30.537 --> 00:00:31.449
schedule usually matters

12
00:00:32.035 --> 00:00:36.068
more than which optimizer you pick. A
constant learning rate plateaus

13
00:00:37.302 --> 00:00:40.857
early and wastes epochs. Step and
exponential decay are simple

14
00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:44.206
but dated. The modern default is cosine
annealing

15
00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:48.353
with a short warmup, and the OneCycle
policy can

16
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:53.187
give you super-convergence at a
surprisingly large rate. Run an LR range

17
00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:57.426
test to find the highest stable value
first. To fight

18
00:00:58.875 --> 00:01:02.117
overfitting, reach into the regularization
toolkit. Dropout zeroes activations

19
00:01:02.797 --> 00:01:05.392
at training time only. Weight decay
penalizes

20
00:01:06.188 --> 00:01:10.380
large weights, while L1 also sparsifies
them. Label smoothing softens those hard

21
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.166
one-hot targets. And early stopping simply
remembers your

22
00:01:14.713 --> 00:01:18.610
best validation loss and restores it.
Normalization deserves its own moment.

23
00:01:20.102 --> 00:01:22.163
Batch norm normalizes across the batch

24
00:01:22.796 --> 00:01:25.038
— great until your batch is

25
00:01:25.389 --> 00:01:26.829
tiny. Layer norm works

26
00:01:27.569 --> 00:01:31.087
per sample and is the default inside
Transformers. Group norm

27
00:01:31.977 --> 00:01:34.456
is the batch-size-independent middle
ground, and instance

28
00:01:34.456 --> 00:01:36.936
norm shows up in style transfer. Finally,

29
00:01:38.382 --> 00:01:39.915
make it fast. Mixed-precision

30
00:01:40.548 --> 00:01:44.635
FP16 buys roughly a one-and-a-half to two
times speedup on modern

31
00:01:44.635 --> 00:01:48.722
GPUs, with a loss-scale optimizer guarding
against underflow. Build the

32
00:01:49.432 --> 00:01:51.478
input side with tf.data — prefetch,

33
00:01:51.866 --> 00:01:53.763
cache, and AUTOTUNE — scale

34
00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:56.473
out with MirroredStrategy, and always
profile before

35
00:01:56.473 --> 00:01:58.797
you guess where the bottleneck is.

36
00:01:59.811 --> 00:02:03.080
Let's benchmark optimizers and schedules —
open the labs.
